    "final_text": "Fixed: `buildUrl` now keeps `retry=0` and `include_archived=false`. The full test suite passes, 10 of 10.\n\n**Root cause:** in `src/url-builder.js`, `serializeQuery` skipped any value that failed `if (!value)`, and `appendQueryValue` did the same for array items with `if (!item)`. Those checks treat `0` and `false` as missing. So the issue's example came out with only `dry_run=true`, and `retry: [0, 1]` also lost its `0`.\n\n**Fix:** both checks now skip only `undefined` and `null`. That's the same check `normalizePath` already uses for path segments. It's a two-line change and the builder API is unchanged.\n\n**Regression tests** (added to `tests/url-builder.test.js`):\n- `buildUrl keeps retry=0 and include_archived=false query values (ISSUE-214)` runs the exact example from the issue.\n- `serializeQuery keeps 0 and false items inside array values` covers the array case.\n- `serializeQuery omits only null and undefined query values` checks that `null` and `undefined` are still left out.\n\nAll three failed before the fix. I also put each old check back one at a time, and each time the matching test failed, so both changed lines are covered.\n\n**One behavior change:** an empty string is no longer dropped. `{ q: '' }` now produces `q=`, which is what `URLSearchParams` does. Callers who relied on `''` being dropped need to pass `undefined` or `null` instead. The third test covers this and the Resolution section in `ISSUE.md` explains it. If you'd rather keep dropping empty strings, it's a one-condition change.\n\nThe changes are not committed; you're still on `main`.",
